From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 15:58:09 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] perf(control): delete the forced render; output wakes the
 renderer now

The throttled `ghostty_surface_render_now` existed to work around a pane
that froze mid-output, blamed on libghostty's wakeup coalescing. That
diagnosis was wrong. On Windows, pty output never woke the renderer at all:
ghostty held the renderer wakeup by value and libxev's IOCP async keeps its
state in the struct, so the copy's notify() set a bool nobody read. Fixed in
ghostty patch 34.

With output driving rendering by itself, forcing a render here is both
unnecessary and expensive - this handler runs on the connection's output
thread, whose blocking *is* the backpressure to the child, and render_now is
a synchronous rebuild and present.

Measured, 8 MB corpus, same session, same probe:

    ghostty, wakeup fixed, no forced render   30.9, 30.9, 30.9  median 30.9
    cascadia                                  30.3, 37.7, 39.0, 32.3

Note the previously published "37.4 = parity" is retracted: it measured a
terminal that was not drawing. See docs/perf/throughput.md.

`_renderNow` stays - six other callers force a redraw after a state change
we make ourselves, which is a different thing from forcing one per chunk of
someone else's output.

unitControl 63/63.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp    | 81 ++++---------------
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h      |  1 -
 2 files changed, 14 insertions(+), 68 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
index 5a6570182..16b7edaaf 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
@@ -80,24 +80,6 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
 
         _initializedTerminal = true;
 
-        // Bounds how often output can force a frame. 8ms is two frames at 60Hz
-        // and one at 120Hz - fast enough that output looks continuous, slow
-        // enough that a flood is not paying for a synchronous present per
-        // chunk of pty output.
-        _renderThrottler = std::make_unique<til::throttled_func<>>(
-            til::throttled_func_options{
-                .delay = std::chrono::milliseconds{ 8 },
-                .debounce = false,
-                .leading = false,
-                .trailing = true,
-            },
-            [weakThis = get_weak()]() {
-                if (const auto self = weakThis.get())
-                {
-                    self->_renderNow();
-                }
-            });
-
         // If the renderer already built a swap chain while we were in
         // ghostty_surface_new, its action fired before anything could act on
         // it. Raising once here costs nothing when the handle is still 0.
@@ -213,10 +195,6 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         }
         _closing = true;
 
-        // Before the surface goes: its destructor cancels any pending trailing
-        // render, which would otherwise fire against a freed surface.
-        _renderThrottler.reset();
-
         _connectionOutputRevoker.revoke();
         _connectionStateRevoker.revoke();
 
@@ -317,53 +295,22 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         ghostty_surface_write_pty_output(_surface,
                                          reinterpret_cast<const uint8_t*>(_utf8Scratch.data()),
                                          _utf8Scratch.size());
-
-        // Then force the frame out.
+        // No render is forced here, and that is deliberate.
         //
-        // Parsing schedules a render through libghostty's `wakeup` async,
-        // which *coalesces by design* (its own comment contrasts it with the
-        // non-coalescing `draw_now`). A notify that lands after the renderer
-        // has already sampled the terminal state is folded into a wake that
-        // has done its work, so the final batch never reaches the screen: the
-        // pane freezes mid-output and stays frozen until something unrelated
-        // wakes it, which is exactly what scrolling did.
+        // Until 2026-08-07 this handler called ghostty_surface_render_now on
+        // every chunk, later throttled to 8ms, to work around a pane that froze
+        // mid-output. The freeze was blamed on libghostty's wakeup coalescing.
+        // It was not: on Windows, pty output never woke the renderer at all,
+        // because ghostty held the renderer wakeup by value and libxev's IOCP
+        // async keeps its state in the struct - so the copy's notify() set a
+        // bool nobody read. Fixed in ghostty patch 34; output now drives
+        // rendering by itself, measured at 321 renders in the second where it
+        // used to manage 2.
         //
-        // Measured: 80 notifies produced fewer than 20 wakes, and drawFrame
-        // stopped being called entirely while the parser kept running.
-        //
-        // It must be render_now rather than draw: ghostty_surface_draw only
-        // re-presents the cells the render thread last built, so forcing it
-        // after new output just redraws stale content - measured, the pane
-        // still froze. render_now rebuilds from terminal state first.
-        //
-        // A pane in a XAML SwapChainPanel has no paint loop, so nothing else
-        // ever forces this. hwnd-host does it on WM_PAINT, which is why three
-        // phases of harness work never surfaced the problem.
-        //
-        // Throttled, though, because forcing it on *every* chunk cost a factor
-        // of nine. This handler runs on the connection's output thread and
-        // render_now is synchronous, so a forced render per chunk put a full
-        // rebuild-and-present in the middle of the path whose blocking is the
-        // backpressure to the child. Measured on an 8 MB corpus: 4.2 MB/s with
-        // a render per chunk, 37.4 MB/s without one, against cascadia's 38.1.
-        //
-        // leading + trailing, not debounced:
-        //   * trailing only, no leading - a leading invocation runs *inline on
-        //                this thread*, so it would go on blocking the
-        //                backpressure path once per interval. Measured:
-        //                leading+trailing at 8ms gave 12.6 MB/s. The cost of
-        //                dropping it is that the first frame of a burst is 8ms
-        //                late, which is not perceptible.
-        //   * trailing - the last chunk always draws, which is the whole point
-        //                of forcing renders here. Without it the coalescing
-        //                wakeup can drop the final batch and the pane freezes
-        //                mid-output, which is the defect this code exists for.
-        //   * not debounced - a sustained flood must not starve the screen;
-        //                every interval gets a frame.
-        if (_renderThrottler)
-        {
-            (*_renderThrottler)();
-        }
+        // Forcing a render here was expensive as well as unnecessary: this runs
+        // on the connection's output thread, whose blocking *is* the
+        // backpressure to the child, and render_now is a synchronous rebuild
+        // and present.
     }
 
     void GhosttyControlCore::_connectionStateChangedHandler()
diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h
index dc6432254..e92454467 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h
@@ -341,7 +341,6 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         // -1 is not a placeholder: SearchBoxControl renders a negative total as
         // "Searching...", which is exactly what is true until they do.
         // Bounds how often pty output forces a frame; see _connectionOutputHandler.
-        std::unique_ptr<til::throttled_func<>> _renderThrottler;
 
         std::wstring _searchNeedle;
         int32_t _searchTotal{ -1 };
